  "content": "• Ecosystems (forest, grassland, desert and aquatic) and ecological terminologies (habitat, niche, population, community, biome, ecosystem, biomass, carrying capacity)\n• Adaptations of organisms to different habitats (xerophytes, mesophytes, hydrophytes, halophytes, desert and aquatic animals)\n• Characteristics of ecosystems and of populations (density, dispersion, growth)\n• Estimating population size (direct count and sampling methods: line transect, belt transect, quadrat, capture-recapture)\n• Factors that affect populations in ecosystems (biotic and abiotic factors)\n• Nutrient cycles and their importance in ecosystems (nitrogen and carbon cycles)\n• Effects of environmental pollution (including global warming and climate change)",
  "learningOutcomes": "By the end of the sub-strand, the learner should be able to:\na) describe the characteristics of different ecosystems in nature,\nb) explain the adaptations of organisms to different habitats,\nc) describe factors affecting population sizes in ecosystems,\nd) estimate population sizes of organisms in ecosystems,\ne) illustrate the nitrogen and carbon cycles in ecosystems,\nf) describe the effects of pollution in the environment,\ng) appreciate the link between environmental pollution and climate change.",
  "coreCompetencies": "• Citizenship: The learner solves problems in the local community as they make posters to sensitise the community about the effects of environmental pollution.\n• Self-efficacy: The learner communicates clearly with others while brainstorming on the causes and impacts of climate change.\n• Communication and collaboration: Learners work in groups to sample school-compound populations, share data and present findings.\n• Critical thinking and problem solving: Learners use evidence to explain why fish are declining in the river and propose restoration actions.\n• Creativity and imagination: Learners design models of nitrogen and carbon cycles and pollution-awareness posters.\n• Digital literacy: Learners use Khan Academy readings, PhET simulations and internet research on pollution in Nairobi and Athi Rivers.",
  "values": "• Integrity: The learner utilises resources prudently while making charts and models to illustrate nitrogen and carbon cycles.\n• Patriotism: The learner demonstrates love for the country while sensitising the community about the effects of environmental pollution.\n• Respect: Learners respect living organisms and the habitats they sample, and one another's views in group work.\n• Responsibility: Learners return sampled areas undisturbed and take responsibility for caring for local water bodies.\n• Ethics: Learners record data honestly and handle organisms and counters ethically.",
  "sep": "• Asking questions and defining problems: Learners generate questions about why fish are declining and add them to the Driving Question Board.\n• Developing and using models: Learners build and revise a cumulative model of the river ecosystem and construct nitrogen and carbon cycle models.\n• Planning and carrying out investigations: Learners carry out direct counts, quadrat, belt transect and capture-recapture investigations in the school compound.\n• Analyzing and interpreting data: Learners calculate population density and estimates, and compare sites.\n• Using mathematics and computational thinking: Learners apply the capture-recapture formula (marked × second sample ÷ recaptured) and density calculations.\n• Obtaining, evaluating and communicating information: Learners use readings, simulations and research to present findings on pollution.\n• Engaging in argument from evidence: Learners argue the cause of fish decline using evidence, correcting the idea that fish are simply old or lack food.",
  "pcis": "• Environmental conservation: Learners study how ecosystems function and propose ways to restore the river ecosystem.\n• Waste Management: The learner appreciates the importance of proper waste management as they sensitise the community about the effects of environmental pollution and their mitigation measures.\n• Prevention of Global Warming: The learner appreciates the need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions while observing and discussing the effects of environmental pollution.\n• Climate change: Learners link pollution and carbon cycle disruption to global warming and climate change.\n• Health: Learners relate polluted water and unpleasant smells to risks to human and animal health.",
