  "focus": "This unit is anchored in a local, Kenyan river that is losing its fish while algae, aquatic weeds and bad smells increase. Learners progress from ecological terms and habitat adaptations, through hands-on population estimation in the school compound, to factors affecting populations, nutrient cycles and pollution, steadily building an explanation and community action plan for the river. Each lesson follows Predict, Observe, Explain, Driving Question Board and Model Building phases.",
  "phenomenon": "Learners watch a Kenyan video (from 2:20 to 4:50) made by local people showing a nearby river that once had many fish and now yields far fewer. At the same time, people notice increased growth of algae and aquatic weeds, and the water sometimes has an unpleasant smell. It is puzzling because the river is still full of life, yet the fish are disappearing. Learners' initial ideas (the fish are old, or they have no food) do not explain why algae and weeds are booming or why the smell appears, so they must investigate how organisms interact with their environment and with human activity.",
  "supportingPhenomena": "• Which area of the school compound has the largest population of a particular organism (e.g. grass, weeds, fallen leaves)?\n• Grass and weeds do not seem to be spread uniformly across the school field, and plants change from open field to shaded area, pathway and garden.\n• Predator and prey numbers rise and fall in a PhET simulation when food becomes scarce.\n• Garbage-choked rivers and effluent in the Nairobi and Athi Rivers, and pollution seen at the neighbouring shopping centre.",
  "drivingQuestion": "DRIVING QUESTION: Why are fish disappearing from a water body that once supported many fish, and what can the community do to restore the ecosystem?\n\nKICD KEY INQUIRY QUESTIONS:\n1. How do organisms interact with their environment?",
  "storylineThread": "Lesson 1: Define ecosystems and ecological terms through a Khan Academy reading; ends with the question: How are organisms adapted to live in different habitats?\nLesson 2: Classify organisms by habitat (xerophytes, mesophytes, halophytes, hydrophytes, desert and aquatic animals) and study their adaptations through a reading; ends with: What are the characteristics of populations?\nLesson 3: Characteristics of populations and direct counting in the school compound (e.g. 10 m × 10 m areas), comparing population density between sites; ends with: Is the distribution of grass uniform throughout the school field?\nLesson 4: Quadrat sampling of grass/weed populations using 1 m × 1 m quadrats placed randomly to estimate population size and density; ends with: How does the population of plants change as environmental conditions change across the school compound?\nLesson 5: Belt transect across a changing habitat (open field, shaded area, pathway, garden) recording plant abundance at regular intervals; ends with: What factors affect population size in an ecosystem?\nLesson 6: Capture-recapture simulation with 100 counters, using estimated population = (marked × second sample) ÷ recaptured; links to estimating animal populations such as fish in the river.\nLesson 7: Factors affecting population size (biotic and abiotic) using a Khan Academy reading and a PhET predator and food scarcity simulation; ends with: How do nitrogen and carbon affect living organisms?\nLesson 8: Discuss and build models of the nitrogen and carbon cycles in ecosystems, linking nutrient enrichment to algae growth; ends with: Does pollution affect organisms?\nLesson 9: Walk to the neighbouring shopping centre to note types of pollution, then research and present on effects of pollution, including garbage rivers and effluent in the Nairobi and Athi Rivers, global warming and climate change, and community solutions to restore the river."
